  "query_preview": "..High pressure is centered south of the Four Corners\nthis evening, with southwest flow in place over Utah. Moisture\ncontinues to spread into western Utah along the periphery of this\nhigh. Latest PWAT analysis has between 0.9-1.1 inches across\nwestern Utah decreasing to around 0.7 inches across eastern Utah.\nWeak ripples embedded in the southwesterlies are also aiding more\nfocused rounds of convection, with one such round moving through\nnorthern Utah at this time.\n\nModels continue to show isolated weak shower activity tonight,\nincreasing again by mid-morning Friday as another weak disturbance\nripples through. As moisture further increases through the day\nFriday, convection will become stronger and increase in coverage \nand become stronger towards the middle part of the afternoon and\ninto Friday evening. Scattered nocturnal shower activity looks \nlike a strong possibility Friday night.\n\nOn Saturday, a storm system crossing the PacNW will bring \nincreased southwesterly flow to the area. The upper jet shifting \ninto Utah ahead of this storm, combined with moisture and \ninstability in place, will bring more widespread convection to the\narea, with increased potential for strong to severe storms. A \nMarginal Risk of severe thunderstorms is already in place for \nnorthern Utah on Saturday. Given the more widespread coverage and \npotential training nature of the storms, the flash flood threat \nwill increase as well, with a Marginal Risk of excessive rainfall \nin place for the entirety of the forecast area for Saturday.\n\nThe trough will cross Idaho Saturday night, resulting in continued\nshowers and thunderstorms through the night. By Sunday afternoon,\nsubsidence behind the trough should bring a marked decrease in \nconvection across at least western Utah, while eastern Utah will \nsee convection gradually decrease in coverage through the day,\ndepending on how quickly said trough progresses eastward.  \n\nThe trough axis will settle well east of the area through the\nearly to middle part of next week. Although PWATs will trend\nlower, additional weak disturbances will continue which will\nmaintain daily shower and thunderstorm activity, although chances\ndecrease by midweek. \n\nTemperatures will trend cooler Friday into Saturday, with high\ntemperatures falling below climatological normals by Saturday.\nThese cooler temperatures are expected to more or less persist\nthrough much of next week.",
